```apex
public with sharing class FollowUpTaskService {
    private static final String SUBJECT_PREFIX = 'Follow up: ';
    private static final String DEFAULT_STATUS = 'Not Started';
    private static final String DEFAULT_PRIORITY = 'Normal';
    private static final Integer FOLLOW_UP_DAYS = 7;
    private static final Integer MAX_SUBJECT_LENGTH = 255;

    /**
     * Creates a follow-up Task for each provided Opportunity Id.
     * Uses bulk partial-success DML (Database.insert with allOrNone = false)
     * to avoid the 150 DML statement governor limit while allowing valid
     * tasks to succeed when others fail validation rules.
     *
     * @param opportunityIds List of Opportunity Ids requiring follow-up tasks.
     * @return Set<Id> The Ids of Opportunities whose follow-up task could not be created.
     */
    public static Set<Id> createFollowUps(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Set<Id> failedOpportunityIds = new Set<Id>();

        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        Set<Id> uniqueOppIds = new Set<Id>(opportunityIds);
        uniqueOppIds.remove(null);

        if (uniqueOppIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        Map<Id, Opportunity> opportunitiesById = new Map<Id, Opportunity>(
            [
                SELECT Id, Name, OwnerId
                FROM Opportunity
                WHERE Id IN :uniqueOppIds
                WITH USER_MODE
            ]
        );

        Date dueDate = Date.today().addDays(FOLLOW_UP_DAYS);
        List<Task> tasksToInsert = new List<Task>();

        for (Id oppId : uniqueOppIds) {
            Opportunity opp = opportunitiesById.get(oppId);
            if (opp == null) {
                failedOpportunityIds.add(oppId);
                continue;
            }

            String fullSubject = SUBJECT_PREFIX + (opp.Name != null ? opp.Name : '');

            tasksToInsert.add(
                new Task(
                    WhatId = opp.Id,
                    OwnerId = opp.OwnerId,
                    Subject = fullSubject.abbreviate(MAX_SUBJECT_LENGTH),
                    ActivityDate = dueDate,
                    Status = DEFAULT_STATUS,
                    Priority = DEFAULT_PRIORITY
                )
            );
        }

        if (!tasksToInsert.isEmpty()) {
            List<Database.SaveResult> saveResults = Database.insert(
                tasksToInsert,
                false,
                AccessLevel.USER_MODE
            );

            for (Integer i = 0; i < saveResults.size(); i++) {
                if (!saveResults[i].isSuccess()) {
                    failedOpportunityIds.add(tasksToInsert[i].WhatId);
                }
            }
        }

        return failedOpportunityIds;
    }
}
```

### What Was Done Differently and Why

1. **Bulkified `@future` Method Invocation (`Set<Id>` instead of a single `Id` per Opportunity):**
   * **Why:** Salesforce enforces a strict governor limit of **50 `@future` calls per transaction**. Calling an `@future` method inside a loop once per Opportunity would immediately fail with a `System.LimitException: Too many future calls: 51` whenever your CPQ integration inserts or updates batches of 200 Opportunities. Instead, the trigger collects all affected `AccountId`s into a single `Set<Id>` and makes **one** `@future` call per trigger invocation. (An overload accepting a single `Id` is also provided for convenience in case single-record callers need it).

2. **Safe Async Invocation Guard (`System.isFuture()` / `System.isBatch()`):**
   * **Why:** An `@future` method cannot be called from another `@future` or Batch context. If a batch job or future method updates Opportunities, calling `@future` unconditionally will throw an unhandled `AsyncException`. When already running asynchronously, the service falls back to executing the recalculation synchronously.

3. **Additional Trigger Events & Reparenting (`after delete`, `after undelete`, and `AccountId` changes):**
   * **Why:** To keep `Rating` accurate at all times, we also need to recalculate when an open Opportunity is deleted, undeleted, or moved from one Account to another (recalculates both the old and new Account).

4. **Aggregate SOQL & Selective DML:**
   * **Why:** Rather than querying every open Opportunity record—which risks hitting the 50,000 SOQL row limit on Accounts with many Opportunities—the service uses a single `GROUP BY AccountId` aggregate query (`MAX(Amount)` and `COUNT(Id)`). Furthermore, it queries the current Account `Rating` and only performs an `update` on Accounts whose `Rating` actually changed, avoiding unnecessary DML, lock contention, and downstream Account trigger recursion.

