public with sharing class LineItemDiscountHandler {
    /**
     * Populates Discount on new OpportunityLineItems when Discount is null,
     * using the standard discount rules from PricingRules in a bulk-safe manner.
     */
    public static void applyStandardDiscounts(List<OpportunityLineItem> lineItems) {
        if (lineItems == null || lineItems.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        List<OpportunityLineItem> linesToProcess = new List<OpportunityLineItem>();
        Set<Id> productIds = new Set<Id>();
        Set<Id> pbeIds = new Set<Id>();

        for (OpportunityLineItem oli : lineItems) {
            // Only apply default discount when Discount was not provided (null).
            // Lines with an explicit Discount (including 0) keep their value.
            if (oli.Discount == null) {
                linesToProcess.add(oli);
                if (oli.Product2Id != null) {
                    productIds.add(oli.Product2Id);
                } else if (oli.PricebookEntryId != null) {
                    pbeIds.add(oli.PricebookEntryId);
                }
            }
        }

        if (linesToProcess.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // In before insert, callers may specify PricebookEntryId without Product2Id.
        // Resolve Product2Id from PricebookEntry if needed.
        Map<Id, Id> pbeToProductId = new Map<Id, Id>();
        if (!pbeIds.isEmpty()) {
            for (PricebookEntry pbe : [
                SELECT Id, Product2Id
                FROM PricebookEntry
                WHERE Id IN :pbeIds
            ]) {
                pbeToProductId.put(pbe.Id, pbe.Product2Id);
                if (pbe.Product2Id != null) {
                    productIds.add(pbe.Product2Id);
                }
            }
        }

        // Single bulk query for all relevant Products
        Map<Id, Product2> productsById = new Map<Id, Product2>(
            [SELECT Id, Family FROM Product2 WHERE Id IN :productIds]
        );

        // Apply the discount rules via PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily
        for (OpportunityLineItem oli : linesToProcess) {
            Id prodId = oli.Product2Id != null ? oli.Product2Id : pbeToProductId.get(oli.PricebookEntryId);
            Product2 prod = productsById.get(prodId);
            if (prod != null) {
                oli.Discount = PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(prod.Family);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### Why I did it differently (Map vs. Nested Loop)

Instead of a nested loop, the implementation below indexes the contacts by their normalized email in a `Map<String, Id>` first, and then performs a single pass over the leads.

**Why:**
1. **CPU Time Limit & Algorithmic Complexity:** A nested loop has $O(L \times C)$ time complexity. With up to 3,000 leads and 3,000 contacts, a nested loop performs up to **9,000,000 iterations** in the worst case (when few or no leads match). In Apex, iterating and performing string manipulations (`trim()`, `toLowerCase()`) millions of times will easily exceed the 10,000 ms synchronous (or 60,000 ms asynchronous) CPU timeout—especially since this runs inside an existing import transaction that already consumes CPU time for DML, triggers, and flows.
2. **$O(L + C)$ Efficiency:** Using a `Map` reduces the complexity to $O(L + C)$—a maximum of **6,000 iterations** total. Each contact's email is normalized only once rather than up to 3,000 times.
3. **Readability & First-Match Requirement:** Using `!contactIdByEmail.containsKey(normalizedEmail)` (or `putIfAbsent`) cleanly preserves the requirement to match the first contact in `contacts` order while keeping the code concise and easy to read.

---

