```apex
public with sharing class LeadContactMatcher {
    public static List<Id> matchByEmail(List<Lead> leads, List<Contact> contacts) {
        List<Id> matchedContactIds = new List<Id>();
        if (leads == null || leads.isEmpty()) {
            return matchedContactIds;
        }

        Map<String, Id> contactIdByEmail = new Map<String, Id>();
        if (contacts != null) {
            for (Contact c : contacts) {
                if (c != null && String.isNotBlank(c.Email)) {
                    String normalizedEmail = c.Email.trim().toLowerCase();
                    if (!contactIdByEmail.containsKey(normalizedEmail)) {
                        contactIdByEmail.put(normalizedEmail, c.Id);
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        for (Lead l : leads) {
            if (l != null && String.isNotBlank(l.Email)) {
                String normalizedEmail = l.Email.trim().toLowerCase();
                matchedContactIds.add(contactIdByEmail.get(normalizedEmail));
            } else {
                matchedContactIds.add(null);
            }
        }

        return matchedContactIds;
    }
}
```

### Changes & Rationale

1. **Single Bulk Queueable Enqueue (Governor Limit Compliance):**
   The request suggested calling `System.enqueueJob(new CaseSurveyJob(c.Id))` inside a loop per Case. In Salesforce, the synchronous governor limit for `System.enqueueJob` is **50 jobs per transaction** (and only 1 in asynchronous contexts). Because agents close cases in bulk from list views and a nightly job closes up to 200 cases at a time, enqueuing inside a loop would throw an uncatchable `System.LimitException: Too many queueable jobs added to the queue: 51`. 
   Instead, `CaseTrigger` collects all qualifying Case IDs into a `Set<Id>` and enqueues **one** `CaseSurveyJob` instance per trigger execution (while also checking `Limits.getQueueableJobs() < Limits.getLimitQueueableJobs()`). For convenience and backwards compatibility, both a single-ID constructor `CaseSurveyJob(Id)` and a bulk constructor `CaseSurveyJob(Set<Id>)` are provided.

2. **Partial Success / Fault Isolation (`Database.insert(tasks, false)`):**
   To preserve the requirement that *"one failure can't affect the others"* within the bulkified job, the `Task` insert uses `Database.insert(tasksToCreate, false)` (`allOrNone = false`). If one record fails validation or a trigger rule, the remaining tasks in the batch will still be created.

3. **Re-querying in Asynchronous Context:**
   `CaseSurveyJob` re-queries the `Case` records (`Id`, `CaseNumber`, `ContactId`, `IsClosed`) when executing asynchronously. This guarantees the job acts on the committed state of the cases and only creates tasks for cases that still have a `ContactId` and remain closed.

