```apex
public with sharing class CaseSurveyJob implements Queueable {
    private final Set<Id> caseIds;

    public CaseSurveyJob(Id caseId) {
        this.caseIds = new Set<Id>();
        if (caseId != null) {
            this.caseIds.add(caseId);
        }
    }

    public CaseSurveyJob(Set<Id> caseIds) {
        this.caseIds = caseIds != null ? new Set<Id>(caseIds) : new Set<Id>();
        this.caseIds.remove(null);
    }

    public void execute(QueueableContext context) {
        if (this.caseIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        List<Task> tasksToCreate = new List<Task>();
        Date dueDate = Date.today().addDays(2);

        for (Case c : [
            SELECT Id, CaseNumber, ContactId
            FROM Case
            WHERE Id IN :this.caseIds
              AND IsClosed = true
              AND ContactId != null
        ]) {
            tasksToCreate.add(new Task(
                WhatId = c.Id,
                WhoId = c.ContactId,
                Subject = 'CSAT survey: ' + c.CaseNumber,
                ActivityDate = dueDate,
                Status = 'Not Started'
            ));
        }

        if (!tasksToCreate.isEmpty()) {
            // Use allOrNone = false so one failing task does not roll back the others
            Database.insert(tasksToCreate, false);
        }
    }
}
```

### Why calling `Messaging.sendEmail` inside a loop is a bad idea

Salesforce enforces a strict governor limit of **10 `Messaging.sendEmail` invocations per synchronous Apex transaction** (`Limits.getEmailInvocations()`). Because your scheduled job passes up to 200 opportunities at once, putting `Messaging.sendEmail` inside the loop will immediately hit a `System.LimitException: Too many Email Invocations: 11` on the 11th opportunity, rolling back the entire transaction so that **no emails are sent at all**.

**The Solution:**
Instead of calling `Messaging.sendEmail` inside the loop, you should:
1. Build a `List<Messaging.SingleEmailMessage>` inside the loop.
2. Call `Messaging.sendEmail(messages, false)` **once** outside the loop. Passing `false` as the second parameter (`allOrNone`) tells Salesforce to allow partial success—if one email fails, the rest of the valid emails in the batch will still be sent, and you can inspect the returned `Messaging.SendEmailResult[]` array to count how many succeeded.

---

